A||||11||SLYGUY||Does this stuff really work..???||||5||
Z||000000||SLYGUY||10-29-2001||04:33 PM||||For quite a few years now I have seen various advertisements from aftermarket lubricant manufacturers about just how good thier product is. I'm sure you guys have seen the commercials from people like Duralube, ZMax and others. <P>My question is this:<P>Does any of this stuff work anything like the way the people promoting them says they do?<BR>Have any of you used it personally?<BR>How did it work/ not work for you?<P>Sly<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ViewImage.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Devotshka||10-29-2001||04:38 PM||||I think this has been covered before in pretty good depth, but lots of those additives supposedly used i think chlorine as an additive because of its lubricating power, but it also has quite the corrosive ability as well.<BR>My personal opinion on it, is if the stuff is so great how come oil companies don't use it?  <BR>I have heard some positive things (besides in the commercials) about some additives, but on the other hand, if i dump $20-50 in a bottle, i'll MAKE myself see an improvement  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Just my opinion though.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||10-29-2001||07:24 PM||||ez answer........NO !...........<P>its just like using one of the 'miracle' waxes, thats guaranteed to make your peeling, chalked, cracked 20 year old paint look 'showroom new'<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 10-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTZ34||10-29-2001||08:44 PM||||I'll have to disagree with Rog on this one.  The two I use are slick50 and prolong.  I have an exploder w/ the SOHC engine which are notorious for needing new cam chain tensioners (which rattle wildly for about a min. upon startup), at about 25K mi.  Sure enough mine started to make noise.  I have always used one or the other of the oil additives and tried slick50 in the truck.  Within 300 mi the noise was completely gone and hasn't returned since with subsequent oil changes.  To date, my truck is the only one (on news groups and in talking with dealers) that the noise went away by itself.  The engine work is covered on an as needed basis recall but I don't like anyone but me working on eng or trans internals for my vehicles.  Prolong was endorsed by Smokey Yunick.  For any of you that know of Smokey, know that guy was the most straight forward character to work on cars and he tested the product in his research garage before he would 'go on TV' to plug it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||SLYGUY||10-30-2001||03:18 AM||||Thanks for the input. I was curious about this stuff as I want to get the most out of the 2.8 that is in my GT. <P>Thanks again...<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||10-30-2001||03:56 AM||||Most of those addatives are just bunk, pure and simple.<P>In my expierence however, Prolong has proven itself to actually work.  I have actually seen it used on an engine (then run for a bit) then all the oil drained.....&lt;sound of Ben wetting his pants&gt;  then they flogged the car for a few hours....  it was pretty impressive.<P>I tossed some into a slightly knocking 2.8 motor, and got nearly 10,000miles out of that engine (wheras the LAST 2.8 to start knocking went only another ~50 miles, then died.)  Both engines were fed with Mobil 1 (10W30  here in 'never freezes' CA) Full Synthetic.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>PS: Stay away from Slick 50, it contains solid (particulite) Teflon, that just dosen't belong in an oil bath, and it clogs your oil filter...<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000006||LZeitgeist||10-30-2001||07:56 AM||||Well, I can't vouch for the claims of any of these 'miracle additives', but I *will* offer my experience with them.<P>Back in 1990, when I got my first Fiero, an '88 Coupe, it had 47,000 miles on it. When I changed the oil at 50,000 miles, I decided to add Slick50, since it was popular at the time, and since the treatment is suggested every 50,000 miles, it was an easy-to-remember 'anniversary gift' to give my car.<P>From that point on, every 50,000 miles, I treated the car with Slick50 again. When I had the treatment in the car, I didn't change the oil again until 5-6,000 miles later, instead of the usual 3,000 mile oil changes (like clockwork) that I always did. My theory was that while it was in there, I'd give it extra time to do its job. <P>The car, now owned by my Dad, currently has 257,000 miles on it, with no internal engine work ever having been needed. Ever. No valve guides, no seals, no reason whatsoever to replace anything inside the motor. (It *did* have a clutch replacement at 100,000 miles, but that didn't require internal engine work - that was outside the engine itself, so I don't count that.) And the car is still going. It's still reliable, and it burns very little oil, considering the high mileage on the car. <P>Did the Slick50 extend the life of the engine? I have no idea. Or was it the fresh oil change every 3,000 miles? Don't know. I am in no way saying that Slick50 prolonged the life of the engine in that car in any way, shape or form... but it doesn't seem to have hurt it, either. I never babied the car - I drove it hard, since it was as close to a 'performance car' as I'd ever owned. Dad babies it a bit, but he enjoys a good romp in it too, so I know the car still experiences a little bit of 'fun' now and again. I'm just relating the facts. You may draw your own conclusions. <P>On a sidenote, I put Slick50 into my Formula as soon as I got it... and I'll continue to do so, every 50,000 miles, for as long as I own the car. Don't know if it helps or not, but it didn't seem to hurt.<P>Just my two cents...<P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001<BR>- Class Winner - 1982 to Present - 2001 Tarheel Tigers All-Pontiac Show||||reg||1
Z||000007||Wannabe||10-30-2001||09:16 AM||||Yes, these products work extremely well for anyone who can arrange for an infomercial and have enough in stock to fill the orders that pour in afterwords. It's just basic psychology - people consider the potential benefit, and look at the cost no being so high and figure "oh well, for $50 it's worth a try". ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula88||10-30-2001||09:42 AM||||Yeah, I can vouch for LZ's coupe not being babied!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  That was the car that got me and him hooked on Fieros almost 10 years ago.  As for the Slick 50 helping?  257K miles is a lot.  I'm sure the religious oil changes played a big part, but if the Slick 50 was bad, I'm sure a problem would have surfaced.  And you don't see many Iron Dukes with over a quarter million miles on them.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||10-30-2001||04:18 PM||||My only personal experience was slick 50 in a lincoln limo. It used no oil at 150K and I had oil changed and slick 50 added. It immediatly started using a quart of oil every 400 miles or so. nothing else done. drained and put back in regular oil and burning stopped. drove it another 100K only doing a valve job once. They also got sued and fed gov made them stop making claims.||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||10-30-2001||05:12 PM||||Consumer Reports tested a lot of those so called wonder additives and found that none did what they claimed. <P>Creative marketing is what you are paying for!<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mickey_Moose||10-30-2001||05:54 PM||||There was a study done some time ago by an engine manufacture to see just how well Slick 50 worked. They took 2 engines off the assembly line, did the usual test of added Slick 50 to one and run the engines then drain all the oil out and run them some more (both engines ran without oil, didn't stop). After runnning them for an hour then stopped them and disassembled both engines to see what they look like inside. What they found was that the engine that had the Slick 50 in, the pistons and heads were burnt, the reason it turns out is that the Slick 50 had plugged the little holes that allow the oil to flow to where is was needed thus starving the engine of oil.<P>Any 'thick' substance will make a noise engine run quieter - check around and see all the 'tricks' that were used (may still be)on used cars lots to quieten noisy drive trains.<P>Tim||||reg||1
